  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nA few rounds of showers and storms today\n\nPotentially a few rounds of showers and storms today as some weak \nshortwaves and MCSs embedded in northwest flow aloft ripple through \nthe region. Scattered upstream evening convection over portions of \nMinnesota and the Dakotas was tied to a plume of modest instability \napproaching 2000 J/kg, with a sharp instability gradient eastward \ninto our forecast area. Through daybreak, that diminishing plume of \ninstability will spill eastward with MUCAPE around 1000 J/kg or \nless, supporting gradually weakening clusters of storms diving \neast/southeast through the forecast area. Because of the rather weak \nforcing, CAMs aren't in the best agreement, but they lend support \nfor most of our area to be impacted by at least one such cluster \nthrough the morning hours, with the greatest chances (50-60%) \ngenerally near and west of the Mississippi River. No severe weather \nexpected with this round of elevated morning convection.\n\nLingering clouds after morning showers and storms will likely temper \nour daytime warming, and the forecast has been trending a smidge \ncooler for today's highs. The main forecast question revolves around \nhow much we manage to destabilize ahead of potentially another round \nof scattered storms this afternoon. This activity would seem to be \nfocused mainly south of I-90 as drier air will be encroaching from \nthe north, but CAMs are not in much agreement as it hinges on how \nmorning convection plays out. Better theta-e convergence will be \nfound south of I-90, and that is where the 00Z HRRR initiates late-\nafternoon scattered storms. While deep layer near-unidirectional \nshear magnitude will be respectable (50+ knots), weak forcing and \nlimited instability thanks to weak mid-level lapse rates should \ninhibit severe potential locally. Forecast soundings show the deeper \n(albeit skinny) CAPE profiles in our far south, suggesting that \nwould be where any healthier updrafts could conceivably manage to \ndevelop, but it looks marginal at best. So, thinking a marginal SPC \nrisk is adequate for potentially an isolated wind threat late this \nafternoon-evening. \n\nToday does not have the look of a widespread soaking rainfall event, \ngiven weak to modest moisture transport, limited warm cloud depths, \nand progressive storm cluster motion. Looking like 0.25 to 0.5\" on \naverage, with some higher pockets perhaps exceeding 1 inch.\n\nPeriodic rain chances Thursday and Friday, then generally drier\n\nUnder broad upper troughing, a drier airmass briefly takes hold for \nWednesday with embedded shortwave activity just clipping our far \nsouthern forecast area. Weak moisture return gradually takes hold \nThursday into Friday, ushering in PWATs around 1.25\". This will \nallow for the return of periodic shower and storm chances as an \nactive northwest flow pattern takes hold aloft. Thursday night into \nFriday brings the highest chances (50-70%). \n\nThis weekend and beyond becomes a little murkier of a forecast for \nrain chances given diverging upper level flow patterns between \nvarious guidance, but overall it looks fairly dry and slightly \ncooler than normal.",
